      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Tapping Formidable Speaker (2/4) to untap Breeding Pool during combat removes a 2-power attacker against an opponent at 6 life. The extra mana could enable post-combat plays, but every point of damage matters when the opponent is this low. Attacking with all three creatures (Formidable Speaker + Badgermole Cub + Multiversal Passage = 5 power) forces the opponent to make tough blocking decisions with Eddymurk Crab.",
      "action_taken": "Tapped Formidable Speaker to untap Breeding Pool instead of attacking with it",
      "better_line": "Attack with Formidable Speaker, Badgermole Cub, and Multiversal Passage to pressure the opponent at 6 life, then cast post-combat spells with remaining mana",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 97,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 103,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Blocked with Badgermole Cub (a valuable creature with mana-doubling ability) instead of an earthbend land creature. The earthbend lands (Multiversal Passage or Breeding Pool) return to the battlefield tapped when they die, making them ideal chump blockers that cost nothing permanent. Badgermole Cub's ability to add additional {G} when tapping creatures for mana synergizes with the Spider Manifestations in hand.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked Eddymurk Crab with Badgermole Cub, permanently losing the 2/2 creature and its mana-doubling ability.",
      "better_line": "Block with Multiversal Passage or Breeding Pool (earthbend land creature), which would return to the battlefield tapped after dying, preserving the Badgermole Cub for its mana ability and future attacks.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 98,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 103,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Llanowar Elves in precombat main phase when it doesn't affect combat math. The opponent's Eddymurk Crab is tapped and can't block, so attacking first with Formidable Speaker and the animated Multiversal Passage is free damage. Playing creatures before combat unnecessarily gives the opponent information and risks the creature being relevant to counterplay.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Llanowar Elves in precombat main phase before attacking",
      "better_line": "Attack first with Formidable Speaker and animated Multiversal Passage for 3 damage (opponent at 3 life), then cast Llanowar Elves and other spells in post-combat main phase",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 101,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 105,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The opponent is at 6 life. Llama4 Truth has Formidable Speaker (2/4) on the board, but it's tapped. Breeding Pool is a land that was earthbent into a creature (from Badgermole Cub's ability earlier). The player chose to attack with only Breeding Pool (likely a 1/1 with a +1/+1 counter, so 1/1) instead of selecting 'All attack' which would send all available creatures. With the opponent at 6 life, maximizing attackers is critical. Formidable Speaker is tapped so it can't attack regardless, but Multiversal Passage (the untapped one) might also be an earthbent land-creature. By not choosing 'All attack', the player potentially missed sending all available creatures and reducing the opponent's life total more aggressively. More critically, the player has Explosive Prodigy in hand which deals damage equal to colors among permanents controlled - this could have been used as burn to finish the opponent. The player also has two Nature's Rhythm to tutor threats. Attacking with just one creature when the opponent is at 6 life and you have multiple potential attackers is a significant missed opportunity.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Breeding Pool (an earthbent land-creature) instead of selecting 'All attack' to send all eligible creatures.",
      "better_line": "Choose 'All attack' to send all untapped creatures into combat, maximizing damage against an opponent at 6 life. Then in second main phase, cast Explosive Prodigy to deal additional damage and potentially close out the game.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 103,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 114,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With 4 untapped lands available, Llama4 only cast Explosive Prodigy (2 mana) when they could have also cast Spider Manifestation (2 mana) in the same turn, developing more board presence with the opponent at 5 life. Additionally, casting Spider Manifestation first would have potentially increased the color count for Explosive Prodigy's Vivid trigger. The Explosive Prodigy's damage likely doesn't kill the 5/5 Eddymurk Crab anyway, making the sequencing and mana usage suboptimal.",
      "action_taken": "Cast only Explosive Prodigy for 2 mana, leaving 2 mana unused",
      "better_line": "Cast Spider Manifestation first (adding board presence and a mana dork), then cast Explosive Prodigy with a potentially higher Vivid count, using all 4 available mana",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 104,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 114,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "DSV3 Analyst cast Sleight of Hand before playing a land. They have Riverpyre Verge and Spirebluff Canal in hand. Spirebluff Canal would enter tapped (they control more than 2 other lands), but Riverpyre Verge could tap for U or R since they control Islands and Mountains. Playing the land first preserves more mana options and doesn't cost anything. Additionally, casting Sleight of Hand adds a sorcery to the graveyard, which would reduce Eddymurk Crab's cost by 1 more — but they should still play the land first to have maximum mana available after the Sleight resolves, in case they want to cast what they find. With 5 life and needing to deploy threats while holding up countermagic, sequencing land before spell is strictly better here.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Sleight of Hand before playing a land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Riverpyre Verge first (enters untapped since they control Islands/Mountains), then cast Sleight of Hand, preserving maximum mana flexibility for the rest of the turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 106,
      "player": "DSV3 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 116,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The opponent is at 5 life. Multiversal Passage (1/1 animated land) and Formidable Speaker (2/4) together deal exactly 3 damage. The Eddymurk Crab is tapped, and the opponent has all lands tapped except two untapped Steam Vents (so 2 mana available). Attacking with both creatures deals 3 damage, putting opponent to 2. But the player only selected Multiversal Passage as an attacker, not both creatures. The reasoning says 'attack with Multiversal Passage and Formidable Speaker' but only Multiversal Passage was chosen. Even attacking with both only deals 3, not lethal, but every point of damage matters at 5 life. Missing 2 damage from Formidable Speaker is significant when the opponent is at 5 life.",
      "action_taken": "Only selected Multiversal Passage as attacker, dealing 1 damage instead of 3.",
      "better_line": "Select 'All attack' to attack with both Multiversal Passage and Formidable Speaker, dealing 3 damage total and putting opponent to 2 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 119,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 125,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "At 4 life vs opponent's 13, attacking with only one Eddymurk Crab instead of both. The opponent has only Gene Pollinator (1/2) as an untapped potential blocker, so attacking with both would likely deal 8-10 damage (opponent to 3-5). At 4 life, the game needs to end quickly, and the clock difference between a 2-turn kill and a 3+ turn kill is significant.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only one Eddymurk Crab, keeping the other back presumably as a blocker",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Eddymurk Crabs to maximize pressure and create a faster clock. With the opponent having minimal blockers and DSV3 Analyst holding countermagic (Spell Pierce, Spell Snare, Spider-Sense) to protect against threats, the aggressive line likely closes the game faster while the defensive posture may not matter if the opponent develops their board further.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 123,
      "player": "DSV3 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 137,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 4 life, Multiversal Passage either enters tapped (losing an untapped mana source) or costs 2 precious life. Island enters untapped for free, which is important for holding up counterspells (Spider-Sense, Spell Snare, Spell Pierce) on the opponent's turn.",
      "action_taken": "Played Multiversal Passage, which likely enters tapped or costs 2 life at a dangerous 4 life total.",
      "better_line": "Play Island, which enters untapped for free, maximizing available mana to hold up countermagic for the opponent's turn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 125,
      "player": "DSV3 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 140,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "DSV3 Analyst cast Spider-Sense targeting its own Spider-Sense on the stack. Spider-Sense counters target instant spell, sorcery spell, or triggered ability - by targeting itself, it counters itself, accomplishing absolutely nothing and wasting the card. The choice presented was between 'Ability' (presumably some triggered ability on the stack) and 'Spider-Sense' itself. If there was a triggered ability on the stack (likely from Badgermole Cub's earthbend or similar), countering that ability would have been the correct play to deny the opponent value.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Spider-Sense targeting itself (Spider-Sense), causing it to counter itself and waste the card for no effect.",
      "better_line": "Target the 'Ability' option to counter whatever triggered ability was on the stack, denying the opponent value from their trigger.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 131,
      "player": "DSV3 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 144,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "With two 5/5 Eddymurk Crabs available and opponent at 8 life with only a 2/2 Badgermole Cub as a blocker, attacking with both crabs forces at least 5 damage through (opponent to 3) or delivers lethal 10 if unblocked. Attacking with only one crab means at most 5 damage and misses the potential lethal.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only one Eddymurk Crab despite two being available",
      "better_line": "Select 'All attack' to attack with both Eddymurk Crabs, presenting lethal damage that the opponent cannot fully block with just a 2/2",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 135,
      "player": "DSV3 Analyst",
      "decision_index": 146,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Opponent is at 4 HP with only one blocker (Eddymurk Crab 5/5). Attacking with only Badgermole Cub means the opponent can block it and take 0 damage, or not block and take 2. Attacking with both Badgermole Cub and Starting Town (earthbent land creature) would force at least 1 damage through since the Crab can only block one, and the land returns to battlefield if it dies due to earthbend.",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only Badgermole Cub",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Badgermole Cub and Starting Town to guarantee damage gets through against a single blocker, putting the opponent closer to lethal range with minimal risk since the earthbent land returns when it dies.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 136,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 150,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 3 life vs opponent's 4 life with no creatures on board, leading with Gene Pollinator (1/2) is less impactful than Spider Manifestation (2/2 that also taps for mana). More importantly, with 7 mana available, the player should be deploying multiple creatures this turn to maximize pressure since the opponent is at 4 life.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Gene Pollinator (1/2 for 1 mana) as the only play before skipping attacks",
      "better_line": "Cast two Spider Manifestations (2/2 each for 2 mana each) plus Gene Pollinator and potentially Explosive Prodigy, deploying maximum board presence to threaten lethal next turn",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 140,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 153,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Player is confused about whose turn it is during their own precombat main phase, thinking the opponent is declaring attackers when they should be selecting their own attackers. With opponent at 4 life, this is a critical turn where attacking and potentially following up with Explosive Prodigy or Spider Manifestation could set up or achieve lethal. The confused reasoning likely led to not attacking or attacking suboptimally.",
      "action_taken": "Player expressed confusion about the turn, thinking it was the opponent's attack step instead of their own precombat main phase, likely leading to a missed or suboptimal attack.",
      "better_line": "Cast Spider Manifestation first (adding a colored permanent for Explosive Prodigy's damage count), then attack with all available creatures. Post-combat, cast Explosive Prodigy targeting Eddymurk Crab or going face if possible, maximizing damage to close out the game with opponent at 4 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 142,
      "player": "Llama4 Truth",
      "decision_index": 155,
    }
  ],
